E('archimedes screw', 'The Archimedes screw is a device for lifting water.', 'It is credited to Archimedes in the 3rd century BC, though it may be older.', 'It is a screw inside a tube, turned by hand or machine.', 'It is still used today for irrigation and in sewage plants.', 'Some modern versions generate electricity.')
E('hagia sophia', 'Hagia Sophia is a vast domed building in Istanbul.', 'It was completed in AD 537 under Emperor Justinian.', 'It was the world’s largest cathedral for nearly a thousand years.', 'It became a mosque in 1453.', 'It became a museum in 1934 and a mosque again in 2020.', 'Its dome seems to float on a ring of windows.')
E('justinian', 'Justinian I was a Byzantine emperor who tried to restore the Roman Empire.', 'He ruled from 527 to 565.', 'His generals reconquered Italy and North Africa.', 'He compiled Roman law into the Justinian Code.', 'He built Hagia Sophia.', 'His wife, Theodora, was a powerful empress.')
E('theodora', 'Theodora was a Byzantine empress and wife of Justinian.', 'She lived from about 497 to 548.', 'She came from humble origins as an actress.', 'During the Nika riots of 532 she reportedly persuaded Justinian not to flee.', 'She championed laws protecting women.')
E('william shakespeare globe|globe theatre', 'The Globe Theatre was the London playhouse where many of Shakespeare’s plays were performed.', 'It was built in 1599.', 'It burned down in 1613 when a cannon set the thatched roof alight.', 'It was rebuilt in 1614 and closed in 1642.', 'A reconstruction opened near the original site in 1997.')
E('gladiators', 'Gladiators were armed fighters who entertained audiences in ancient Rome.', 'Gladiator games began around 264 BC and ended in the 5th century AD.', 'Most were slaves, prisoners or criminals, though some volunteered.', 'They fought in different styles, with nets, tridents, swords and shields.', 'Not every fight ended in death.', 'Successful gladiators became celebrities.')
E('roman legion|legionaries', 'A Roman legion was the main unit of the Roman army.', 'Legions were used from the Republic through the Empire.', 'A legion had around 5,000 soldiers.', 'Soldiers carried a short sword (gladius) and a large shield (scutum).', 'They could form a “tortoise” formation with shields overlapping.', 'They built roads and forts as well as fighting.')
E('knights|chivalry', 'Knights were mounted warriors in medieval Europe.', 'They were prominent from about the 11th to the 15th century.', 'A boy trained as a page, then a squire, before being knighted.', 'Chivalry was a code of honour, courtesy and protecting the weak.', 'Tournaments and jousts let knights show their skill.', 'Gunpowder weapons made heavy armoured knights less important.')
E('castles', 'Castles were fortified homes built by medieval lords.', 'Most European castles were built between the 11th and 15th centuries.', 'Early ones were wooden motte-and-bailey castles.', 'Later ones had stone walls, towers and moats.', 'Cannons eventually made castles easy to breach.', 'Many were later turned into grand houses.')
E('monasteries|monks', 'Monasteries were communities of monks devoted to religious life.', 'Christian monasticism spread in Europe from the 6th century.', 'Saint Benedict wrote a famous rule for monks.', 'Monks copied books by hand, preserving ancient texts.', 'Monasteries ran schools, hospitals and farms.', 'Henry VIII dissolved England’s monasteries in the 1530s.')
E('universities history|oldest university', 'Universities developed in medieval Europe.', 'The University of Bologna, founded in 1088, is often called the oldest in continuous operation in the Western world.', 'Oxford was teaching by 1096.', 'The University of al-Qarawiyyin in Morocco was founded in 859.', 'Medieval students studied Latin, logic, law and theology.')
E('knights templar|templars', 'The Knights Templar were a Christian military order founded during the Crusades.', 'They were founded around 1119.', 'They protected pilgrims travelling to Jerusalem.', 'They became very rich and ran an early banking system.', 'The French king Philip IV had them arrested in 1307.', 'The order was dissolved in 1312, inspiring many legends.')
E('inquisition|spanish inquisition', 'The Spanish Inquisition was a tribunal to enforce Catholic orthodoxy in Spain.', 'It lasted from 1478 to 1834.', 'It targeted converted Jews and Muslims suspected of secretly keeping their faith.', 'It used torture and public trials called autos-da-fé.', 'Thousands were executed.')
E('pilgrims|mayflower|thanksgiving', 'The Pilgrims were English settlers who sailed to America on the Mayflower.', 'They landed at Plymouth, Massachusetts, in 1620.', 'They sought religious freedom.', 'About half died in the first winter.', 'The Wampanoag people helped them survive.', 'The harvest feast of 1621 is remembered as the first Thanksgiving.')
E('jamestown', 'Jamestown was the first permanent English settlement in North America.', 'It was founded in 1607, in Virginia.', 'Settlers struggled with disease and starvation.', 'Tobacco became its cash crop.', 'The first enslaved Africans in English North America arrived nearby in 1619.')
E('emancipation proclamation', 'The Emancipation Proclamation was an order by Abraham Lincoln to free enslaved people in Confederate states.', 'It took effect on 1 January 1863.', 'It applied only to areas in rebellion.', 'It allowed Black men to join the Union army.', 'Slavery was fully abolished by the 13th Amendment in 1865.', 'Juneteenth marks the day news of freedom reached Texas in 1865.')
E('juneteenth', 'Juneteenth commemorates the end of slavery in the United States.', 'It marks 19 June 1865.', 'That day, Union troops announced freedom to enslaved people in Galveston, Texas.', 'It became a US federal holiday in 2021.')
E('ellis island', 'Ellis Island was the main immigration station in New York.', 'It operated from 1892 to 1954.', 'About 12 million immigrants passed through it.', 'Inspectors checked arrivals for illness.', 'It is now a museum.')
E('hoover dam', 'The Hoover Dam is a giant concrete dam on the Colorado River.', 'It was built between 1931 and 1936.', 'It is on the border of Nevada and Arizona.', 'It created Lake Mead.', 'It generates hydroelectric power for millions.')
E('empire state building', 'The Empire State Building is a famous skyscraper in New York.', 'It was completed in 1931.', 'It was built in just over a year.', 'It was the world’s tallest building until 1970.', 'It is 381 metres tall to its roof, 443 metres with its antenna.')
E('burj khalifa|tallest building', 'The Burj Khalifa in Dubai is the tallest building in the world.', 'It opened in 2010.', 'It is about 828 metres tall.', 'It has over 160 storeys.', 'You can see it from nearly 100 kilometres away.')
E('channel tunnel', 'The Channel Tunnel links England and France under the sea.', 'It opened in 1994.', 'It is about 50 kilometres long.', 'Its undersea section is the longest in the world.', 'Trains carry passengers and cars through it.')
E('apollo 13', 'Apollo 13 was a Moon mission that had to be aborted after an explosion.', 'It launched on 11 April 1970.', 'An oxygen tank exploded on the way to the Moon.', 'Astronaut Jack Swigert reported, “Houston, we’ve had a problem.”', 'The crew used the lunar module as a lifeboat.', 'All three astronauts returned safely.')
E('laika', 'Laika was a Soviet dog, the first animal to orbit Earth.', 'She flew aboard Sputnik 2 on 3 November 1957.', 'She had been a stray in Moscow.', 'She died within hours from overheating.', 'Monuments to her stand in Russia.')
E('neil armstrong biography', 'Neil Armstrong was an American astronaut and the first person to walk on the Moon.', 'He lived from 1930 to 2012.', 'He was a naval aviator and test pilot.', 'He flew on Gemini 8 in 1966.', 'He was famously modest about his fame.', 'He later taught engineering.')
E('tank man', '“Tank Man” is the unidentified man who stood in front of a column of tanks in Beijing.', 'It happened on 5 June 1989, the day after the Tiananmen crackdown.', 'He blocked the tanks for several minutes.', 'His identity and fate remain unknown.', 'The image is one of the most famous photographs ever.')
E('watergate scandal', 'Watergate was a political scandal that forced President Nixon to resign.', 'It began with a break-in at the Watergate building in June 1972.', 'Reporters Bob Woodward and Carl Bernstein investigated it.', 'Their secret source was nicknamed “Deep Throat”.', 'Tapes revealed Nixon tried to cover it up.', 'He resigned on 9 August 1974.')
E('martin luther reformer', 'Martin Luther was a German monk and theologian who started the Protestant Reformation.', 'He lived from 1483 to 1546.', 'He posted his Ninety-five Theses in Wittenberg in 1517, according to tradition on a church door.', 'He refused to recant at the Diet of Worms in 1521.', 'He married a former nun, Katharina von Bora.')
E('john calvin|calvinism', 'John Calvin was a French Protestant reformer.', 'He lived from 1509 to 1564.', 'He led the church in Geneva.', 'He taught predestination, the idea that God decides who will be saved.', 'His ideas spread to Scotland, the Netherlands and New England.')
E('erasmus', 'Erasmus was a Dutch Renaissance humanist scholar.', 'He lived from about 1466 to 1536.', 'He published a new Greek edition of the New Testament.', 'He criticised church corruption with satire.', 'The EU’s student exchange program is named after him.')
